Yes, and so do riding gloves if they aren't fumble.

Checking the source code reveals that, indeed, they should, at least
according to the comment on the code in steed.c (chance is the computed
percent odds, at this point riding skill has already been factored in):

	if (Confusion || Fumbling || Glib)
	    chance -= 20;
	else if (uarmg &&
		(s = OBJ_DESCR(objects[uarmg->otyp])) != (char *)0 &&
		!strncmp(s, "riding ", 7))
	    /* Bonus for wearing "riding" (but not fumbling) gloves */
	    chance += 10;
	else if (uarmf &&
		(s = OBJ_DESCR(objects[uarmf->otyp])) != (char *)0 &&
		!strncmp(s, "riding ", 7))
	    /* ... or for "riding boots" */
	    chance += 10;

So:
Confusion, fumbling and whatever "Glib" is (stunned?) decrease the
chance by 20%,
If your worn gloves' description contains "riding ", chance increases
by 10%, and if your boots' description contains "riding " it's also a
10% bonus.

Notice that this code is done in the form of an if/else if block, so
being confused or fumbling (like from fumble gloves) will ensure that
the bonus from riding equipment doesn't apply, and also wearing both
riding gloves and riding boots cannot provide a cumulative bonus.

Another interesting note is that the snow boots ice bonus and the
riding equipment saddle bonus are implemented in a fairly kludgy way,
by doing a comparison check with the item's description to see if it
contains the right text.  The riding equipment bonus is added in
steed.c when a saddle is applied by looking at the description of those
worn equipment slots (if I'm reading it right).  The snow boots check
gets its own function in o_init.c, find_skates(), to figure what kind
of object is snow boots in this game, but that item type is found using
strcmp.

